  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Deadlocks: Detection, Cause, and Cure",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Postgres does not prevent deadlocks; it detects them.",
            "It kills one transaction as the victim.",
            "The detector picks it: not by age, not by priority.",
            "Your code cannot assume which side dies."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "ERROR:  deadlock detected",
            "DETAIL:  Process 123 waits for ShareLock",
            "  on transaction 456; blocked by",
            "  process 789.   -- SQLSTATE 40P01"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Detection Is Lazy",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A waiter runs cycle detection only after `deadlock_timeout` (1s).",
            "Checking the wait graph is expensive.",
            "Lower it: faster detection, CPU burnt on every lock wait.",
            "Raise it: deadlocks hide behind latency."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Cheapest Alarm",
          "items": [
            "`log_lock_waits = on`.",
            "Logs any wait past `deadlock_timeout`.",
            "Finds contention before it becomes a deadlock."
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Four Causes",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Cause",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "inconsistent lock order",
              "always lock lower id first"
            ],
            [
              "lock upgrade: SELECT then UPDATE",
              "`FOR UPDATE` on the first read"
            ],
            [
              "FK / unique-index inserts",
              "order writes by parent key"
            ],
            [
              "bulk UPDATE, no order",
              "lock rows in id order first"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT id FROM accounts WHERE id IN (1,2)",
            "  ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE;  -- global order",
            "UPDATE accounts SET bal=bal-100 WHERE id=1;",
            "UPDATE accounts SET bal=bal+100 WHERE id=2;"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Cause 3 detail: concurrent child inserts touching one parent.",
            "Or unique-index inserts, each waiting on the other's uncommitted row.",
            "Cause 4 detail: physical visit order differs per session."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Deadlocks: Detection, Cause, and Cure (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Retry & Shrink The Window",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "40P01, like 40001, means roll back and run again.",
            "Retry with jitter so the pair does not re-collide.",
            "Keep txns short; acquire all locks up front.",
            "Never hold a lock across an HTTP call or user think-time."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Diagnosis Workflow",
          "text": "Read the DETAIL line for both PIDs, find both statements in the log, compare their lock acquisition order, then impose one ordering on every code path."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Locks On Numbers You Invent",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Locks on arbitrary 64-bit keys of your choosing.",
            "Postgres enforces mutual exclusion, attaches no meaning.",
            "Reach for them when the thing to serialize is not a row."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Typical Uses",
          "items": [
            "A cron job that must run once.",
            "A migration runner.",
            "An external API call.",
            "Leader election across app servers."
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Key Shapes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT pg_advisory_lock(42);      -- bigint",
            "SELECT pg_advisory_lock(7, 42);   -- ns, id",
            "SELECT pg_try_advisory_lock(42);  -- bool",
            "-- false means someone else holds it"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Two-int form is a handy namespace + id.",
            "Stable keys: `hashtext('my-job')`.",
            "Or `('x'||md5(name))::bit(64)::bigint`.",
            "Beware collisions across unrelated features: namespace them."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Session vs Transaction Scope",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "pg_advisory_lock",
                "lines": [
                  "held until unlock",
                  "or session disconnects",
                  "survives COMMIT and ROLLBACK",
                  "re-entrant: 2 locks need 2 unlocks"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "pg_advisory_xact_lock",
                "lines": [
                  "released at end of txn",
                  "cannot unlock manually",
                  "safer by default",
                  "pooler-safe"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Pooler Footgun",
          "text": "With PgBouncer in transaction pooling mode, session-level advisory locks leak onto whichever backend served you. Use `pg_advisory_xact_lock` there, always."
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Advisory Locks: Application Locks, Database-Enforced",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Singleton Worker",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "IF NOT pg_try_advisory_lock(",
            "     hashtext('nightly-report')) THEN",
            "  RETURN;  -- another server owns it",
            "END IF;"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Exactly one instance across every app server.",
            "Shared variants exist: `pg_advisory_lock_shared`.",
            "And `pg_advisory_xact_lock_shared` for many-readers-one-writer.",
            "Session re-entrancy is a common leak source."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Inspect & Limits",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT classid, objid, pid, granted",
            "FROM pg_locks",
            "WHERE locktype = 'advisory';",
            "-- classid/objid hold your key halves"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "They join the same deadlock detector as row locks.",
            "You can deadlock an advisory lock against a row lock.",
            "They prevent concurrent code paths, not data anomalies.",
            "Only callers that agree to ask for the lock are constrained."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Row Or Number?",
          "text": "`FOR UPDATE` on a real row is better whenever a row exists. Advisory locks win when the resource is abstract or spans tables."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Guardrail Timeouts",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Setting",
            "Caps"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`lock_timeout`",
              "wait for a lock"
            ],
            [
              "`statement_timeout`",
              "total statement time"
            ],
            [
              "`idle_in_transaction_...`",
              "abandoned txn"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SET lock_timeout = '2s';",
            "ALTER TABLE jobs ADD COLUMN tries int;",
            "-- blocked DDL fails fast, no queue",
            "-- ACCESS EXCLUSIVE conflicts with all"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Advisory Locks: Application Locks, Database-Enforced (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Migration waiting on ACCESS EXCLUSIVE queues every later query.",
            "That is how one ALTER takes the site down.",
            "`pg_blocking_pids(pid)` gives the blocking chain directly.",
            "Join `pg_locks` to `pg_stat_activity` for the full picture."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Decision Tree",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flowchart",
          "spec": {
            "title": "what is the invariant?",
            "rows": [
              [
                "n0"
              ],
              [
                "b0",
                "b1",
                "b2",
                "b3"
              ],
              [
                "c0_0",
                "c0_1"
              ],
              [
                "c1_0"
              ],
              [
                "c2_0"
              ],
              [
                "c3_0"
              ]
            ],
            "nodes": {
