              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Never default to Serializable everywhere out of fear.",
            "Never default to Read Committed everywhere out of habit.",
            "Choose per transaction, deliberately."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Queue Without Locks",
          "text": "`SKIP LOCKED` skips rows another txn holds — the standard work-queue trick. `NOWAIT` errors instead of queuing, good for interactive UIs."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Retry Contract",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "SQLSTATE",
            "Meaning",
            "Retry?"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "40001",
              "serialization failure",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "40P01",
              "deadlock",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "23505",
              "unique violation",
              "no"
            ],
            [
              "23503",
              "FK violation",
              "no"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Both retryables mean: re-run the whole txn unchanged.",
            "Up to ~5 attempts, exponential backoff with jitter.",
            "Full ROLLBACK before each retry.",
            "A metric counting retries per endpoint.",
            "23xxx are logic errors: never retry them as transient."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Idempotence Or Nothing",
          "text": "The retried body runs again from the top. Side effects must be idempotent or moved outside the transaction."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Transaction Hygiene",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Open late, commit early.",
            "Never hold one across network I/O or user input.",
            "Set `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout`.",
            "A forgotten BEGIN otherwise pins the vacuum horizon forever."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Playbook: Choosing, Retrying, and Monitoring",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Monitoring",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT pid, now()-xact_start AS age, query",
            "FROM pg_stat_activity",
            "WHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL",
            "ORDER BY age DESC;  -- long transactions"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`xact_commit` vs `xact_rollback`: abort rate.",
            "`pg_stat_database.deadlocks`: deadlock count.",
            "`wait_event_type = 'Lock'`: who is stuck now.",
            "`n_dead_tup` and `age(datfrozenxid)`: the bloat tax."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Level Summary",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Level",
            "Snapshot",
            "Retries",
            "Allows"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Read Committed",
              "per statement",
              "none",
              "lost update, write skew"
            ],
            [
              "Repeatable Read",
              "per txn",
              "concurrent update",
              "write skew"
            ],
            [
              "Serializable",
              "per txn + SSI",
              "dependency cycles",
              "nothing"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Prove It",
          "items": [
            "Prefer UNIQUE, CHECK, EXCLUDE, FK over txn tricks.",
            "A constraint holds regardless of level or app bug.",
            "Test for real: isolation tester `.spec` files.",
            "And `pgbench` custom scripts for contention."
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Final Rule",
          "text": "Pick the weakest isolation level that still makes the invariant impossible to break, then prove it with a concurrent test."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Part 2 of POSTGRES TXN & LOCKING. Your Postgres default silently allows lost updates and write skew, and it never tells you. This 10-page field handbook walks the whole stack: MVCC internals and xmin/xmax, what each of the three real isolation levels still permits, row locks and SKIP LOCKED queues, deadlock causes with their fixes, and advisory locks for the things that aren't rows. Ends with a decision tree, a retry contract, and the monitoring queries to prove you got it right.",
